#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092017 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4daa6bc-1c9a-5c23-8032-4d70435d9388" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Vector Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "90308076-a0ef-5e6a-900d-2ef4c8107907" :question/math-academy-id "e-5466" :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{v}$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/assets/371521f0acd44811e109c10460e0cc99eb41d256f37f209dcefc003fd78ea70e.png)\nWhat is the original vector $\\mathbf{v}?$" :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/assets/c95b308a6f55de3acc8a2bf446b38ac10500dd506795898b0892952147d5c9e7.png)\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by $T^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-15-(-20)}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -10 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -10 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -10 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092018 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "7969785b-fc71-50f4-9d73-da5599630b6e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5467" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/assets/7b8cdc56144f73cd1e3510c155508478767d0bf058e854e39f52bcad0f2e0413.png)\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle $S'$ under the action of $\\mathbf T.$ The triangle $S'$ has vertices $(4,3),(4,10),$ and $(-4,-3).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(1,0)$\n\n- $(4,-3)$\n\n- $(-1,0)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of $S'\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062093/assets/e7f7558d370d506a87ea9466703f9f9ac2e4ea970572b67c06d2114cdbc0f6fa.png)\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by $T^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4-(-3)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 14 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 28 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(1,0),(2,4),(-1,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342358 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4daa6bc-1c9a-5c23-8032-4d70435d9388" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Vector Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0d4a3a4-a3e1-5175-8e62-06768740bc7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342360} {:answer/id #uuid "e8c5fe59-183a-5571-b76e-013eed8c2b0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342361} {:answer/id #uuid "719c8388-5346-52f6-8c39-a73c2e22c1aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342362} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0925ae-b319-553a-beb5-2051ae2adcbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342363} {:answer/id #uuid "92858c10-64a9-54ad-a354-4d909c680e28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342364}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8c5fe59-183a-5571-b76e-013eed8c2b0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342361} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4498873-bf08-5e50-b29c-7b1ec7b20a2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342359}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1593d047-1fe0-507f-9ebe-ac5fb8102d96" :question/math-academy-id "q-12271" :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$" :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have\n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{6-(-2)}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342365 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3de669da-53ae-5ce6-94a7-67651a3b66de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342367} {:answer/id #uuid "021c77a7-20b6-56cb-a589-d7376f324270" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342368} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb35dde-9872-50e3-a16d-d79e59265220" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342369} {:answer/id #uuid "31619a0b-779b-57bc-8cdc-ca9135d760b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342370} {:answer/id #uuid "1710ef27-b32b-543f-b8f2-2fbb5ed9f641" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "021c77a7-20b6-56cb-a589-d7376f324270" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342368} :answer-field/id #uuid "06a3e583-052a-59fe-8a5b-76b29d32a62a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "185591ef-5105-5530-b9a8-c36a84a0c979" :question/math-academy-id "q-12275" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe quadrilateral $Q$ is mapped to the quadrilateral ${Q}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The quadrilateral ${Q}^{′}$ has vertices $(0,4),(-4,4),(12,4),$ and $(7,3).$ Which of the following are vertices of $Q?$\n\n- $(-2,4)$\n- $(1,-2)$\n- $(4,4)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${Q}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 & 12 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $Q$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 & 12 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 & 12 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 & 12 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2-0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 & 12 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 & 12 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -8 & 8 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 8 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -4 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $Q$ are $(-2,4),(-4,4),(4,4),(2,3).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342372 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4daa6bc-1c9a-5c23-8032-4d70435d9388" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Vector Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39eecfd6-5ba7-55b4-9240-a85e6f7ba387" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342374} {:answer/id #uuid "52fd7a27-fd27-5f14-bf6c-ed3de847b79b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342375} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc4ba1d-ea82-5bd1-a161-5726b1e92ef8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342376} {:answer/id #uuid "b4aadee4-ea30-5ee9-ae49-5fe9c0c5d315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342377} {:answer/id #uuid "6437755a-397b-5ffe-b124-c998c8b33f7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fc4ba1d-ea82-5bd1-a161-5726b1e92ef8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342376} :answer-field/id #uuid "de346f2f-6410-5d48-86ea-ae66cbea21e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f8bf1e1-8ee0-5cfe-8f68-75d9105c47f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-159975" :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$" :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4-3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342379 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56b2bfc4-5db7-5beb-b11c-01f580b80f0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342381} {:answer/id #uuid "289e1bb8-78e4-512b-a997-ec8d2ba62cce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342382} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ea5414-74ee-5621-80d3-7abf66809eac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342383} {:answer/id #uuid "47dbaa6d-989e-5646-8bdc-ecf66142e339" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342384} {:answer/id #uuid "06a80391-e0a7-59be-babb-c7ecd8f97ab8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06a80391-e0a7-59be-babb-c7ecd8f97ab8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342385} :answer-field/id #uuid "23505b1a-91f6-57fd-8808-92228c825b28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dea2572d-5d35-55a7-a857-659b44f2fca5" :question/math-academy-id "q-159977" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(3,4),(0,2),$ and $(7,10).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(0,1)$\n- $(2,3)$\n- $(1,0)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4-2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(1,1),(-1,2),(2,3).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd33a1f1-4257-513e-881e-9dfb20e07697" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342388} {:answer/id #uuid "789b225e-8acf-5879-a606-67f117073887" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342389} {:answer/id #uuid "a38cfd29-720a-5323-81bb-a5a2fd5a5327" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342390} {:answer/id #uuid "0369bfc1-2f13-5deb-91bd-ffe9ecbb22ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342391} {:answer/id #uuid "5b7fba57-9ad4-5740-8e8b-a340bc4c64e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0369bfc1-2f13-5deb-91bd-ffe9ecbb22ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342391} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b760981-ba25-5ada-a7c7-c946e31d0169" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bcd87f78-58f5-5d01-80bd-895e270f3b86" :question/math-academy-id "q-159978" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(3,2),(7,5),$ and $(2,2).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(-2,2)$\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(0,0)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{9-8}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(1,0),(1,1),(-2,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342393 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4daa6bc-1c9a-5c23-8032-4d70435d9388" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Vector Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed0a3b23-bd22-5511-b811-a7c3a990b198" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed0a3b23-bd22-5511-b811-a7c3a990b198" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342395} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e1f4996-3844-581e-9eef-dcb0e2eaa57c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342394} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb8ce314-640e-53ae-b914-ed22bfff43d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb8ce314-640e-53ae-b914-ed22bfff43d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342397} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f6b3cd4-9348-50ca-9749-843649e50279" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "45408e7c-610c-556e-a6fe-6de1f9d2a829" :question/math-academy-id "q-317443" :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\nv={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the inverse of $T$:\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{(5)(1)-(6)(2)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo the original vector is\n\n$$\nv=-\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{7} \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342398 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4daa6bc-1c9a-5c23-8032-4d70435d9388" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Vector Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1589d319-2f67-5d65-989f-145e93672ff8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342400} {:answer/id #uuid "9cb1ddce-8af5-5019-8c8f-7c44949223c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1589d319-2f67-5d65-989f-145e93672ff8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342400} :answer-field/id #uuid "b36de599-ebb4-5041-ada7-132cc8112377" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342399} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f65b0715-9280-5485-8fdb-e226c4c82bd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f65b0715-9280-5485-8fdb-e226c4c82bd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342403} :answer-field/id #uuid "78a53d31-32be-5e8f-8805-77be51949813" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a6b06e9-e6b9-5a73-ab99-fc0c92c99fd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-317507" :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(2)(-1)-(1)(1)}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-3)}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283853 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4daa6bc-1c9a-5c23-8032-4d70435d9388" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Vector Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a021e344-a3d3-5d81-8d0d-71a821517f14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283855} {:answer/id #uuid "d824e97e-c767-5491-9b27-57197b58eefb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d824e97e-c767-5491-9b27-57197b58eefb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283856} :answer-field/id #uuid "46cc790d-e372-586c-9565-30757ab035be" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283854} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2bd5967-0a06-5e5f-90ed-f30f5110edfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2bd5967-0a06-5e5f-90ed-f30f5110edfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283858} :answer-field/id #uuid "42cc3774-b7a2-5d8e-ab2e-f8af50711a34" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4055556b-4590-59c0-ae15-eb16a5349d24" :question/math-academy-id "q-317509" :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(2)(3)-(5)(1)}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}45 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342404 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbb496a6-bdd6-5c5d-acc9-7c29122660b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342406} {:answer/id #uuid "a589d169-a1ee-5150-ac2c-095f321aba93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342407} {:answer/id #uuid "771e051c-6b45-5bc6-b7d2-e86074f82c75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342408} {:answer/id #uuid "d0579c9e-54e5-5b1e-af23-52ef4a525a94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342409} {:answer/id #uuid "07e0c7b7-fbee-5b27-bcbf-4a24349c7fb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342410}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0579c9e-54e5-5b1e-af23-52ef4a525a94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342409} :answer-field/id #uuid "a154e459-ecf9-5d97-be47-1d6d77cb2c87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9079775-d7f3-54f6-b251-e9776a901ea7" :question/math-academy-id "q-9253" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(-1,0),(1,0),$ and $(0,1).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(1,0)$\n- $(-1,0)$\n- $(0,2)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-2-0}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(1,0),(-1,0),(0,\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302719 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f763be17-07f9-5202-929d-0569f45dad17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302721} {:answer/id #uuid "c0b43e18-f376-51a5-b559-7275d6dd9404" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302722} {:answer/id #uuid "a4690e49-98cb-500a-8a24-2a5450a75394" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302723} {:answer/id #uuid "776c663b-0fbb-54b2-9cf3-976f43c93aef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302724} {:answer/id #uuid "1498a4c9-87e4-5710-a9a2-9ef8d1924f63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f763be17-07f9-5202-929d-0569f45dad17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302721} :answer-field/id #uuid "5584d02d-df96-50c4-89a3-b26deebd1b8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "965eaccb-b8c7-5f4b-a1ea-f7ee3a9791ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-9262" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe parallelogram $P$ is mapped to the parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ has vertices $(2,-5),(3,-6),(4,-8),$ and $(3,-7).$ Which of the following are vertices of $P?$\n\n- $(1,3)$\n- $(3,3)$\n- $(2,3)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${P}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $P$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0-1}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $P$ are $(1,2),(3,3),(4,4),(2,3).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}]}
